WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.889
Welcome to episode five.

00:00:01.929 --> 00:00:04.943
Your body runs on chemical energy too.

00:00:04.983 --> 00:00:09.685
Today we treat food as a fuel:
where its energy comes from,

00:00:09.725 --> 00:00:14.789
and how to calculate and compare
the energy in different foods.

00:00:15.133 --> 00:00:17.114
Quick check from last episode.

00:00:17.154 --> 00:00:20.481
Is cellular respiration
exothermic or endothermic?

00:00:20.521 --> 00:00:24.186
And where does the energy
for photosynthesis come from?

00:00:24.226 --> 00:00:25.370
Pause and answer.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:38.106
Respiration is exothermic: glucose
reacting with oxygen releases energy.

00:00:38.146 --> 00:00:42.453
Photosynthesis is endothermic, and
its energy is absorbed from sunlight.

00:00:42.493 --> 00:00:45.692
Today we follow the energy
that respiration releases.

00:00:46.666 --> 00:00:52.015
In respiration, glucose reacts with
oxygen to form carbon dioxide and water,

00:00:52.055 --> 00:00:55.844
just like the combustion of
a fuel, but in many small,

00:00:55.884 --> 00:00:58.894
enzyme-controlled steps
instead of a flame.

00:00:58.934 --> 00:01:04.819
The overall equation and the overall energy
change are the same as burning glucose.

00:01:05.199 --> 00:01:06.641
Look at the oxygen.

00:01:06.681 --> 00:01:10.073
In oxygen gas, its
oxidation number is zero.

00:01:10.113 --> 00:01:13.972
In carbon dioxide and in
water, it's negative two.

00:01:14.012 --> 00:01:18.261
A decrease in oxidation number
means oxygen is reduced.

00:01:18.301 --> 00:01:21.537
At the same time, the glucose is oxidised.

00:01:21.577 --> 00:01:24.072
Respiration is a redox reaction.

00:01:24.466 --> 00:01:25.913
Energy is conserved.

00:01:25.953 --> 00:01:30.077
The energy released doesn't
disappear and isn't created.

00:01:30.117 --> 00:01:34.017
Some is captured to power
muscles and cell processes,

00:01:34.057 --> 00:01:38.924
but a large share is released as
heat, which keeps your body warm.

00:01:38.964 --> 00:01:42.979
So not all of the released
energy becomes useful work.

00:01:43.932 --> 00:01:47.184
Food labels give the mass
of each macronutrient.

00:01:47.224 --> 00:01:50.612
To estimate energy, we use
average energy factors:

00:01:50.652 --> 00:01:56.167
carbohydrate provides about sixteen
kilojoules per gram, protein about seventeen,

00:01:56.207 --> 00:01:58.019
and fat about thirty-seven.

00:01:58.059 --> 00:02:02.859
Fat provides more than twice as much
energy per gram as the other two.

00:02:03.199 --> 00:02:07.549
Here's an example: a muesli bar
label, per one hundred grams,

00:02:07.589 --> 00:02:13.522
lists sixty grams of carbohydrate, eight
grams of protein and fifteen grams of fat.

00:02:13.562 --> 00:02:16.153
These are illustrative label values.

00:02:16.532 --> 00:02:19.897
Each nutrient contributes
its mass times its factor.

00:02:19.937 --> 00:02:25.070
Carbohydrate: sixty grams times sixteen
kilojoules per gram is nine hundred and

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:26.183
sixty kilojoules.

00:02:26.223 --> 00:02:30.177
Protein: eight times seventeen
is one hundred and thirty-six.

00:02:30.217 --> 00:02:34.303
Fat: fifteen times thirty-seven
is five hundred and fifty-five.

00:02:34.343 --> 00:02:37.420
The grams cancel each
time, leaving kilojoules.

00:02:37.799 --> 00:02:43.723
Add the three contributions: one thousand
six hundred and fifty-one kilojoules per

00:02:43.763 --> 00:02:45.033
one hundred grams.

00:02:45.073 --> 00:02:50.125
Notice that fat, at only fifteen
grams, supplies a third of the total.

00:02:50.165 --> 00:02:53.002
That's the effect of its larger factor.

00:02:53.998 --> 00:02:59.186
Labels report energy on different bases:
per one hundred grams, per serving,

00:02:59.226 --> 00:03:00.905
and sometimes per packet.

00:03:00.945 --> 00:03:03.381
To convert, keep the units attached.

00:03:03.421 --> 00:03:08.374
A forty-five gram serving is forty-five
hundredths of one hundred grams.

00:03:08.732 --> 00:03:14.293
So the serving energy is one thousand six
hundred and fifty-one kilojoules per one

00:03:14.333 --> 00:03:16.888
hundred grams, times forty-five grams.

00:03:16.928 --> 00:03:20.098
The grams cancel, and we
divide by one hundred:

00:03:20.138 --> 00:03:24.168
about seven hundred and
forty-three kilojoules per serving.

00:03:24.532 --> 00:03:30.962
The energy per gram is the same whether
you eat a crumb or a whole packet:

00:03:31.002 --> 00:03:36.033
about sixteen point five
kilojoules per gram for this bar.

00:03:36.073 --> 00:03:39.094
Per gram is a property of the food.

00:03:39.134 --> 00:03:42.631
Per serving depends on how much you eat.

00:03:42.998 --> 00:03:43.864
Checkpoint.

00:03:43.904 --> 00:03:50.369
If you eat two servings instead of one,
what happens to the energy you take in,

00:03:50.409 --> 00:03:53.703
and what happens to the energy per gram?

00:04:00.132 --> 00:04:05.825
The energy you take in doubles, to about
one thousand four hundred and eighty-six

00:04:05.865 --> 00:04:06.604
kilojoules.

00:04:06.644 --> 00:04:09.081
The energy per gram doesn't change:

00:04:09.121 --> 00:04:13.085
it's still about sixteen point
five kilojoules per gram.

00:04:14.064 --> 00:04:19.618
The sixteen kilojoules per gram for
carbohydrate is a rounded nutritional factor.

00:04:19.658 --> 00:04:25.142
It's an average for the energy the body
can obtain from carbohydrates in typical

00:04:25.182 --> 00:04:25.891
foods.

00:04:25.931 --> 00:04:28.902
A measured heat of
combustion is different.

00:04:28.942 --> 00:04:31.352
For pure glucose burned completely,

00:04:31.392 --> 00:04:35.274
it's about two point eight
thousand kilojoules per mole.

00:04:35.314 --> 00:04:39.895
Dividing by the molar mass, one
hundred and eighty grams per mole,

00:04:39.935 --> 00:04:43.436
gives about fifteen point
six kilojoules per gram.

00:04:43.797 --> 00:04:49.082
The two numbers are close, but they're
answers to different questions,

00:04:49.122 --> 00:04:51.212
reported on different bases.

00:04:51.252 --> 00:04:55.396
In a question, use the data
and the basis you're given.

00:04:55.436 --> 00:05:00.265
Don't quietly swap a food factor
for a molar heat of combustion,

00:05:00.305 --> 00:05:02.130
or the other way around.

00:05:03.064 --> 00:05:07.288
Practice question Q zero
nine is worth four marks.

00:05:07.328 --> 00:05:09.631
Pause the video and try it.

00:05:22.064 --> 00:05:27.446
What's asked: the energy in one
serving, in kilojoules and in joules.

00:05:27.486 --> 00:05:33.418
The label gives masses per one hundred
grams, and a serving is thirty grams.

00:05:33.458 --> 00:05:38.094
So we'll find the energy per
one hundred grams, then scale.

00:05:38.464 --> 00:05:39.536
Predict first.

00:05:39.576 --> 00:05:43.348
A serving is under a third
of one hundred grams,

00:05:43.388 --> 00:05:49.582
so its energy should be a bit under a
third of the per-one-hundred-gram value.

00:05:49.930 --> 00:05:54.885
Carbohydrate: forty-eight point zero
grams times sixteen is seven hundred and

00:05:54.925 --> 00:05:56.377
sixty-eight kilojoules.

00:05:56.417 --> 00:06:00.724
Protein: twelve point zero times
seventeen is two hundred and four.

00:06:00.764 --> 00:06:05.459
Fat: eighteen point zero times
thirty-seven is six hundred and sixty-six.

00:06:05.499 --> 00:06:10.819
Total: one thousand six hundred and
thirty-eight kilojoules per one hundred grams.

00:06:11.197 --> 00:06:13.635
Now scale to one thirty-gram serving:

00:06:13.675 --> 00:06:19.128
one thousand six hundred and thirty-eight
times thirty point zero over one hundred

00:06:19.168 --> 00:06:22.142
gives four hundred and
ninety-one point four,

00:06:22.182 --> 00:06:25.156
about four hundred and
ninety-one kilojoules.

00:06:25.196 --> 00:06:27.875
That's just under a third, as predicted.

00:06:28.264 --> 00:06:31.094
In joules, multiply by one thousand:

00:06:31.134 --> 00:06:37.631
four hundred and ninety-one point four
kilojoules is four point nine one times ten

00:06:37.671 --> 00:06:39.864
to the power of five joules.

00:06:39.904 --> 00:06:44.528
A larger unit to a smaller one,
so the number gets bigger.

00:06:44.864 --> 00:06:49.039
Marks: two for the weighted sum,
one for the serving factor,

00:06:49.079 --> 00:06:51.638
and one for the conversion to joules.

00:06:51.678 --> 00:06:54.869
The traps: using the
whole ninety-gram packet,

00:06:54.909 --> 00:06:59.927
which gives about one thousand four
hundred and seventy-four kilojoules,

00:06:59.967 --> 00:07:05.446
or writing four hundred and ninety-one
joules when the value is in kilojoules.

00:07:06.397 --> 00:07:08.320
Which food has more energy?

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:13.044
That question isn't complete until
you say: more energy per what?

00:07:13.084 --> 00:07:16.678
Here are two foods with
illustrative label values.

00:07:16.718 --> 00:07:21.403
Nuts: two thousand five hundred
kilojoules per one hundred grams.

00:07:21.443 --> 00:07:26.458
Cooked pasta: six hundred and fifty
kilojoules per one hundred grams.

00:07:26.797 --> 00:07:29.989
Per one hundred grams,
the nuts win easily,

00:07:30.029 --> 00:07:34.012
because they're rich in fat
and contain little water.

00:07:34.397 --> 00:07:38.443
But a typical serving of nuts
might be twenty-five grams,

00:07:38.483 --> 00:07:41.883
which is six hundred and
twenty-five kilojoules.

00:07:41.923 --> 00:07:45.897
A serving of pasta might be
two hundred and fifty grams,

00:07:45.937 --> 00:07:50.269
which is one thousand six hundred
and twenty-five kilojoules.

00:07:50.309 --> 00:07:52.316
Per serving, the pasta wins.

00:07:52.664 --> 00:07:54.512
Both statements are true.

00:07:54.552 --> 00:07:56.930
They answer different questions.

00:07:56.970 --> 00:08:02.595
In an exam, state the basis, show the
numbers for both foods on that basis,

00:08:02.635 --> 00:08:05.204
and only then make the comparison.

00:08:06.196 --> 00:08:09.743
Practice question Q zero
ten is worth five marks.

00:08:09.783 --> 00:08:15.892
Before you calculate anything, decide which
two comparison bases the question wants.

00:08:15.932 --> 00:08:17.909
Then pause and work it out.

00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:37.880
The two bases are energy per one
hundred grams and energy per serving.

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:42.864
Food A per one hundred grams: fifty
times sixteen, plus ten times seventeen,

00:08:42.904 --> 00:08:47.848
plus twelve times thirty-seven, gives
one thousand four hundred and fourteen

00:08:47.888 --> 00:08:48.570
kilojoules.

00:08:48.610 --> 00:08:52.701
Food B: thirty-five times sixteen,
plus twenty times seventeen,

00:08:52.741 --> 00:08:57.882
plus fifteen times thirty-seven, gives
one thousand four hundred and fifty-five

00:08:57.922 --> 00:08:58.722
kilojoules.

00:08:58.996 --> 00:09:02.084
So per one hundred
grams, food B is higher.

00:09:02.124 --> 00:09:03.320
Now the servings.

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:05.720
Food A's serving is eighty grams:

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:11.683
one thousand four hundred and fourteen
times zero point eight zero is one thousand

00:09:11.723 --> 00:09:15.174
one hundred and thirty-one
point two kilojoules.

00:09:15.214 --> 00:09:20.919
Food B's serving is fifty grams: one
thousand four hundred and fifty-five times

00:09:20.959 --> 00:09:26.559
zero point five zero is seven hundred
and twenty-seven point five kilojoules.

00:09:26.930 --> 00:09:30.425
So food B has more energy
per one hundred grams,

00:09:30.465 --> 00:09:33.666
but the food A serving
contains more energy.

00:09:33.706 --> 00:09:39.190
Marks: two for the per-one-hundred-gram
values, two for the serving values,

00:09:39.230 --> 00:09:43.020
and one for stating both
comparisons on their bases.

00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:49.060
The trap is comparing an eighty-gram serving
with a fifty-gram serving and calling

00:09:49.100 --> 00:09:51.236
it a comparison of the foods.

00:09:52.196 --> 00:09:52.784
To recap.

00:09:52.824 --> 00:09:57.037
Respiration oxidises food just
as combustion oxidises a fuel,

00:09:57.077 --> 00:10:01.917
and the energy is conserved, partly
as useful work and partly as heat.

00:10:01.957 --> 00:10:05.334
Food energy is estimated
with factors of sixteen,

00:10:05.374 --> 00:10:08.611
seventeen and thirty-seven
kilojoules per gram.

00:10:08.651 --> 00:10:14.019
Keep units attached when scaling, and
always state the basis of a comparison.

00:10:14.396 --> 00:10:15.515
Closing recall.

00:10:15.555 --> 00:10:20.963
How much energy do twenty grams of
fat provide, using the food factor?

00:10:29.396 --> 00:10:33.994
Twenty grams times thirty-seven kilojoules
per gram is seven hundred and forty

00:10:34.034 --> 00:10:34.648
kilojoules.

00:10:34.688 --> 00:10:38.572
Next episode, we return to fuels
and balance combustion equations,

00:10:38.612 --> 00:10:40.515
including incomplete combustion.
